---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LeadForm from '../../components/LeadForm.astro';
import { STICKY_ABU_DHABI } from '../../data/sticky-cta';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Abu Dhabi Property Investment: Free Shortlist"
  description="Abu Dhabi property shortlist for foreign investors. Saadiyat, Yas and Al Reem often price below Dubai equivalents. Golden Visa AED 2M. Licensed partner review."
  stickyCta={STICKY_ABU_DHABI}
>
  <section class="site-container py-12 md:py-16 max-w-3xl">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <span class="badge-pill mb-4">Abu Dhabi</span>
      <h1 class="mb-4">Abu Dhabi property investment</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-[var(--color-muted)]">
        Momentum market with designated freehold zones. We shortlist by yield (Al Reef) vs appreciation (Saadiyat) vs lifestyle (Yas).
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <a href="#lead-form" class="btn-primary">Get Abu Dhabi options</a>
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose mb-10">
      <p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Nine freehold investment zones under Law 13/2019. Gross yields run roughly 5 to 9% depending on district, and foreign buyers made up close to 88% of recent Aldar sales volume.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/abu-dhabi-property-investment-guide/">Abu Dhabi investment guide</a> · <a href="/compare/abu-dhabi-vs-dubai-investment/">Abu Dhabi vs Dubai</a></p>
      <p>Compare markets: <a href="/gcc-rental-yields/">GCC rental yields hub</a> · <a href="/invest-abu-dhabi-off-plan/">Abu Dhabi off-plan</a></p>

      <h2>Where Abu Dhabi differs from Dubai</h2>
      <p>
        The two markets are often compared on price alone, which misses what actually changes for a foreign buyer. Abu Dhabi ownership sits inside nine designated Investment Zones under Law 13/2019, so eligibility attaches to the plot rather than to the emirate. A unit inside a zone and one immediately outside it can differ by the entire ownership right, which is why the first check is always the Department of Municipalities and Transport record rather than the sales brochure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Registration also differs. Abu Dhabi charges a 2% transfer fee against Dubai's 4%, with mortgage registration adding roughly 0.25%. That gap is real money on an AED 2 million purchase, but it comes with a thinner secondary market: marketing periods run longer, and the buyer pool for resale is smaller than in comparable Dubai communities. Treat the fee saving as compensation for slower liquidity rather than as free return.
      </p>

      <h2>Three theses, three districts</h2>
      <p>
        Most Abu Dhabi enquiries resolve into one of three positions, and the right district follows from which one you actually hold.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Yield first.</strong> Al Reef and the mid-market mainland communities price lowest per square foot and print the highest gross figures, with tenant demand tied to mainland employment rather than to island lifestyle.</li>
        <li><strong>Capital growth first.</strong> Saadiyat carries the cultural-district premium and the deepest owner-occupier demand, at yields that rarely justify the entry on income alone.</li>
        <li><strong>Family use first.</strong> Yas and Al Raha trade some yield for school access, amenity density and shorter commutes, which is what keeps tenants on 24 to 36 month leases.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Al Reem sits between the first and third: mid-rise stock, mainland-adjacent, and the district with the most consistent search interest of the group. Detail on each sits in the <a href="/guides/abu-dhabi-property-investment-guide/">Abu Dhabi investment guide</a> and the individual area pages.
      </p>

      <h2>What a realistic Abu Dhabi model includes</h2>
      <p>
        Gross yields across the emirate commonly quote between 5% and 9% depending on district. Net is the number worth underwriting, and getting there means subtracting service charges quoted in AED per square foot for the specific building, a management fee of roughly 5% to 8% if you are not local, and a vacancy allowance that reflects a shallower tenant pool than Dubai's. On mid-market stock that path typically removes two to three percentage points from the headline.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tawtheeq is the Abu Dhabi equivalent of Ejari, and registered contracts are the only rent evidence worth modelling from. Portal asking rents in Abu Dhabi run above achieved rents in the same way they do in Dubai, and the gap is wider on islands where the listed inventory turns over slowly.
      </p>

      <h2>Golden Visa from Abu Dhabi stock</h2>
      <p>
        The AED 2 million threshold applies here as it does in Dubai, assessed on registered value and administered through the federal system. The practical difference is menu rather than mechanics: Investment Zone stock is concentrated across a handful of islands, so a fixed AED 2 million budget faces perhaps a dozen realistic communities rather than the sixty-plus freehold areas Dubai offers. Screen for zone eligibility first, then for yield. Full detail in <a href="/guides/abu-dhabi-golden-visa-property/">Abu Dhabi Golden Visa property</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What we send back</h2>
      <p>
        Invest Gulf is independent research, not a brokerage. Tell us the budget, the horizon and which of the three theses above you actually hold, and we return three to five Abu Dhabi options with the zone status confirmed, a net yield estimate rather than a gross one, and the specific checks we would want answered before a deposit. Where a purchase proceeds, enquiries go to RERA-licensed partners.
      </p>
    </div>
    <LeadForm title="Abu Dhabi property shortlist" subtitle="Yield · family · capital growth" context="Abu Dhabi property investment landing" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
